🚨 The Hidden Clock in AdTech: Why Meta and Google are Deleting Your Production History

If your data science models or executive dashboards rely on pulling long-term performance trends directly from ad network APIs, your pipeline is built on a countdown timer.
Many developers and data engineers don't realize that Meta Ads and Google Ads enforce a hard 37-month data retention cap on their backend servers for granular, atomic data.
• Meta Ads API: The legacy date_preset: 'lifetime' parameter is deprecated and replaced with maximum. If you query daily or device-level breakdowns older than 3 years, the API returns an empty set.
• Google Ads API: While aggregated monthly records persist longer, requesting granular data split by daily segments (segments.date) or keyword coordinates older than 37 months throws a hard DateRangeError.
If your system relies on on-the-fly API querying, you are permanently losing raw historical data every single day on a rolling clock [1].

The Solution: Granular Data Warehousing

To bypass the 37-month server ceiling, engineering teams must transition to an Incremental Granular Data Warehouse Pipeline.
"Granular" means extracting data at its lowest functional atomic level—broken down by single days, explicit hardware devices, and distribution streams (Facebook vs. Instagram). By capturing these high-density rows and streaming them into an independent analytical engine (like PostgreSQL, BigQuery, or Snowflake), you secure structural control over your dataset.
Once the data hits your warehouse, it is yours permanently. You can safely run 5- to 6-year lookbacks, compile custom attribution models, and run predictive budgeting routines without hitting third-party API walls.

🛠️ The Production Blueprint: Daily Ingestion Framework

The most resilient approach is deploying an automated ingestion microservice (via cron job or cloud function) that runs nightly at 2:00 AM. Instead of broad, historical pulls that trigger API rate limits, the service queries only "yesterday's" data in a tight, predictable payload and appends it to your storage.
Here is the decoupled architecture used to handle this task reliably:

1. Meta Ads: Asynchronous Chunking Pipeline

Because synchronous calls with heavy daily breakdowns frequently trigger gateway timeouts, we initiate an asynchronous export job, poll the operational status, and consume the cursor pagination.
javascript
import fetch from 'node-fetch';

async function runMetaIncrementalSync(startDate, endDate) {
  // 1. Initialize an asynchronous report job to safeguard against HTTP timeouts
  const initUrl = `https://facebook.com`;
  const response = await fetch(initUrl, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({
      access_token: process.env.META_ACCESS_TOKEN,
      level: 'campaign',
      time_increment: 1, // Restructures payload into distinct daily granular rows
      time_range: { since: startDate, until: endDate },
      fields: 'campaign_id,campaign_name,spend,impressions,clicks',
      breakdowns: 'publisher_platform' // Splits records by Facebook, Instagram, etc.
    })
  });
  
  const { report_run_id } = await response.json();
  
  // 2. Poll the execution state until Meta's server completes the dataset compilation
  let isReady = false;
  while (!isReady) {
    await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 5000));
    const statusCheck = await fetch(`https://facebook.com{report_run_id}?access_token=${process.env.META_ACCESS_TOKEN}`);
    const statusData = await statusCheck.json();
    if (statusData.async_status === 'Job Completed') isReady = true;
  }

  // 3. Traverses the data payload using native cursor-based pagination mechanics
  let fetchUrl = `https://facebook.com{report_run_id}/insights?access_token=${process.env.META_ACCESS_TOKEN}&limit=500`;
  while (fetchUrl) {
    const dataResponse = await fetch(fetchUrl);
    const dataPayload = await dataResponse.json();
    
    // PRODUCTION ACTION: Batch insert rows into your staging table
    // db.collection('meta_raw_history').insertMany(dataPayload.data);
    
    fetchUrl = dataPayload.paging?.next || null;
  }
}
Use code with caution.

2. Google Ads: Streamed Ingestion Framework

Google Ads can return millions of segmented keyword and matching rows. Instead of buffering the complete payload into system memory (which risks out-of-memory crashes), we use gRPC streaming to pipeline data records directly from the Google network edge into our target storage engine.
javascript
import { GoogleAdsServiceClient } from 'google-ads-api';

const client = new GoogleAdsServiceClient({
  client_id: process.env.GADS_CLIENT_ID,
  client_secret: process.env.GADS_CLIENT_SECRET,
  developer_token: process.env.GADS_DEV_TOKEN
});

async function streamGoogleAdsToStorage(customerId, startDate, endDate) {
  const gaqlQuery = `
    SELECT segments.date, campaign.id, campaign.name, segments.device, metrics.clicks, metrics.impressions, metrics.cost_micros
    FROM campaign
    WHERE segments.date BETWEEN '${startDate}' AND '${endDate}'
  `;

  const stream = client.searchStream({ customer_id: customerId, query: gaqlQuery });

  // Stream raw data packets sequentially to guarantee a flat application memory profile
  stream.on('data', (chunk) => {
    for (const row of chunk.results) {
      const normalizedRow = {
        date: row.segments.date,
        campaignId: row.campaign.id,
        campaignName: row.campaign.name,
        device: row.segments.device,
        clicks: row.metrics.clicks,
        impressions: row.metrics.impressions,
        spend: parseFloat(row.metrics.cost_micros) / 1000000 // Transforms internal Google Micros to decimal currency
      };
      // PRODUCTION ACTION: Stream write output pipeline directly to target data lake
      // dataLakeStream.write(normalizedRow);
    }
  });
}
Use code with caution.

The Takeaway

Stop treating SaaS ad platforms like static database tables. They are highly aggressive operations caches. If you aren't pulling data granularity into a platform you control, you are dropping your engineering metrics on the floor. Build your ingestion layer today before your history zero-outs.

